---
import:
  - practice_domain
  - web_agent_contract
description: "Python LiveKit voice agent worker for the dental voice receptionist MVP. Joins a LiveKit room when a simulated call starts, greets the patient, answers questions about services, staff, and hours in English, and books appointments through a STT to LLM to TTS pipeline."
---

***definitions***
- :VoiceAgent: is the voice-agent worker implemented by this module. :VoiceAgent: connects to a LiveKit room when a :Call: starts, speaks to the :Patient: in English through a speech-to-text, language-model, text-to-speech pipeline, answers questions about :Practice:, :Service:, and :Staff:, books :Appointment: entries on behalf of the :Patient:, and persists transcript and summary back into the :Call: record.
- :WebApp: is the running web application that :VoiceAgent: communicates with over HTTP during a :Call:. :WebApp: hosts the practice, service, staff, availability, appointment, and call HTTP APIs that :VoiceAgent: reads and writes.

***implementation reqs***

- :VoiceAgent: is implemented in Python 3.11 or newer. The project must use type hints throughout and pass `mypy --strict` on its own modules.
- :VoiceAgent: is built with the LiveKit Agents Python SDK (`livekit-agents`). The renderer must verify the current SDK API against the LiveKit documentation; do not infer method names or class structure from training-data memory.
- The agent worker uses three plugins from the LiveKit Agents ecosystem: `livekit-plugins-deepgram` for speech-to-text, `livekit-plugins-openai` for the language model, and `livekit-plugins-elevenlabs` for text-to-speech. These plugins are wired into the LiveKit Agents `VoicePipelineAgent` (or its current equivalent — verify against the SDK) in that order.
- The language-model engine uses the OpenAI Chat Completions API with model `gpt-4o-mini` by default. The model id is read from environment variable `OPENAI_MODEL` and defaults to `gpt-4o-mini` if unset.
- The text-to-speech engine uses the ElevenLabs voice whose voice id is read from environment variable `ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID`. If the variable is unset the worker fails fast at startup with a clear error message.
- Dependency management uses `uv` with a `pyproject.toml` at `agent/pyproject.toml`. A `uv.lock` file is committed.
- :VoiceAgent: never accesses the database directly. All persistence and lookups go through :WebApp:'s HTTP API using the `httpx` async client. The :WebApp: base URL is read from environment variable `WEB_API_BASE_URL` (no default — required).
- All external services are configured exclusively via environment variables: `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET` (LiveKit Cloud), `DEEPGRAM_API_KEY` (Deepgram), `OPENAI_API_KEY` (OpenAI), `ELEVENLABS_API_KEY` and `ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID` (ElevenLabs), and `WEB_API_BASE_URL` (the running :WebApp:). Local development reads them from a `.env` file in the `agent/` folder via `python-dotenv`. A committed `agent/.env.example` documents every required variable.
- Source code is organized under `agent/src/voice_agent/` with the following modules: `main.py` (worker entry point and CLI), `session.py` (per-:Call: agent session — joins room, loads context, drives the pipeline, finalizes the call), `web_client.py` (typed async wrapper around :WebApp:'s HTTP endpoints), `tools.py` (LLM tool definitions for availability lookup, booking creation, and any other agent-callable actions), `prompts.py` (the system prompt template and any supporting prompt fragments), `config.py` (env-var loading with validation).
- The system prompt is kept tight and is composed at session start by injecting only the loaded :Practice:, :Service:, and :Staff: data plus the agent's role description. Tool definitions exposed to the LLM are limited to the minimum needed for the current conversation phase. The agent does not include unrelated context to minimize latency.
- The agent must respond in English only. Any non-English LLM output is reissued as an English clarification request rather than spoken to the :Patient:.
- Coding style is enforced with `ruff` for both lint and format. CI-time `ruff check` and `ruff format --check` must pass with no warnings.
- :VoiceAgent: should include :UnitTests: covering `web_client.py`, `tools.py`, and the session-lifecycle behavior in `session.py` (with the LiveKit pipeline and HTTP calls mocked).

***test reqs***

- :UnitTests: of :VoiceAgent: are written in Python and run with pytest.
- :UnitTests: live under `tests/` (a sibling of `src/`) and mirror the structure of `src/voice_agent/`, with filenames of the form `test_<module>.py`.
- :UnitTests: cover at minimum: `web_client.py` (every HTTP method exercised with happy-path and error-path responses), `tools.py` (every tool exercised with happy-path and at least one edge-case input), and `session.py` (the session lifecycle — joining, context loading, greeting, transcript appending, finalize — with the LiveKit pipeline mocked).
- :UnitTests: never make real network calls. All :WebApp: HTTP calls are mocked using `httpx.MockTransport`. All LiveKit Agents SDK pipeline objects, plugin clients (Deepgram, OpenAI, ElevenLabs), and audio I/O are mocked via `unittest.mock` or pytest fixtures.
- Async test functions use `pytest-asyncio` in `auto` mode so `async def` test functions are picked up without explicit decoration.
- :UnitTests: are executed by the script `test_scripts/run_unittests_python.sh`, which receives the rendered agent project's folder as its first positional argument and is fully self-contained — every invocation stages the project into its own working folder, runs `uv sync` to install dependencies into a project-local `.venv` based on `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`, then invokes `uv run pytest tests/` with the project's default pytest configuration. The script must be safe to run multiple times back-to-back without state leaking between runs.

***functional specs***
- Implement the entry point for :VoiceAgent: as a long-running worker process.
  - The worker reads the LiveKit server URL, API key, and API secret from server-side configuration.
  - The worker reads the base URL of :WebApp: from server-side configuration.
  - The worker connects to the LiveKit server and registers itself as an agent listener for rooms whose name begins with the prefix `call-`.
  - For each such room, :VoiceAgent: joins the room as a participant with identity `agent-<call_id>`, where `<call_id>` is the substring of the room name after the `call-` prefix.
  - On joining a room, :VoiceAgent: subscribes to all audio tracks published by other participants and publishes its own outgoing audio track.
- Load practice context at the start of each :Call:.
  - Immediately after joining a `call-<call_id>` room and before producing any audio, :VoiceAgent: fetches three resources from :WebApp: in parallel: `GET /api/practice`, `GET /api/services`, and `GET /api/staff`.
  - The three responses are held in memory for the lifetime of the :Call: as the agent's grounding context.
  - If any of the three requests fails (network error or non-200 status), :VoiceAgent: leaves the room and does not greet the :Patient:.
- Greet the :Patient: at the start of each :Call:.
  - Once the practice context has been loaded, :VoiceAgent: speaks one greeting to the :Patient: through its outgoing audio track, generated by the configured text-to-speech engine.
  - The greeting text is the value of :Practice:.GreetingTemplate. If GreetingTemplate is missing or empty, the fallback greeting text is `"Hello, thank you for calling. How can I help you today?"`.
  - The greeting is spoken exactly once per :Call:.
- Answer :Patient: questions about the practice using the loaded context.
  - While in a :Call:, :VoiceAgent: passes each :Patient: utterance transcribed by the speech-to-text engine to the language-model engine to generate a spoken response.
  - The response is grounded only in the in-memory practice context loaded at session start (:Practice:, the :Service: list, and the :Staff: list).
  - The agent can answer questions about: the practice name and address; the phone number to reach the practice (using :Practice:.PhoneDisplay); the opening and closing times for each day of the week (from :Practice:.HoursOfOperation); the services offered, their durations in minutes, prices in US dollars, and descriptions (from the :Service: list); the names, roles, and bios of staff members (from the :Staff: list).
  - The agent always responds in English.
  - The agent never asks the :Patient: about insurance, medical history, or payment, and never volunteers information that is not present in the loaded context.
- Append each conversational turn to the :Call: transcript via the web app.
  - Each time the speech-to-text engine produces a final transcription of a :Patient: utterance, :VoiceAgent: sends `POST /api/calls/<call_id>/transcript` to :WebApp: with body `{"speaker": "patient", "text": "<transcription>"}`.
  - Each time :VoiceAgent: emits a spoken response, it sends `POST /api/calls/<call_id>/transcript` to :WebApp: with body `{"speaker": "agent", "text": "<spoken text>"}`.
  - Transcript writes are best-effort: any non-200 response is logged but does not interrupt the call.
- Offer to book an :Appointment: when the :Patient: asks about scheduling.
  - When the :Patient: signals an intent to book (for example phrases like "I'd like to schedule", "can I book", or "do you have time for a cleaning"), :VoiceAgent: identifies the requested :Service: by matching the :Patient:'s words against the loaded :Service: list by `name` (case-insensitive substring match). If no :Service: matches, the agent asks the :Patient: to clarify which service they want.
  - Once a :Service: is identified, :VoiceAgent: derives a date range to search: if the :Patient: gave a specific date or week the agent uses that; otherwise the agent uses the next 7 days starting from the current local date.
  - :VoiceAgent: calls `GET /api/availability?serviceId=<id>&from=<YYYY-MM-DD>&to=<YYYY-MM-DD>` with the identified :Service: and the derived date range.
  - From the returned array, :VoiceAgent: selects up to two slots (the two earliest) and proposes them to the :Patient: by speaking the staff name, day-of-week, date, and start time of each. If the array is empty the agent tells the :Patient: no slots are available in that window and offers to widen the search.
- Confirm a booking and create the :Appointment: via the web app.
  - When the :Patient: selects one of the proposed slots, :VoiceAgent: asks the :Patient: for their first and last name. If the spoken name is unclear or empty the agent re-asks once.
  - With a chosen slot and a non-empty name, :VoiceAgent: sends `POST /api/appointments` to :WebApp: with body `{"serviceId": "<id>", "staffId": "<id>", "patientPhone": "<phone>", "patientName": "<name>", "startAt": "<YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM>"}` (where `patientPhone` is read from the :Call:'s `patientPhone`).
  - On HTTP 201 the agent stores the returned :Appointment: `id` for the current :Call: and confirms the booking out loud, speaking the service name, staff name, and start date and time.
  - On HTTP 409 with `{"error": "slot unavailable"}` the agent tells the :Patient: that slot is no longer available and re-runs the availability lookup for the same date range, then re-proposes slots.
  - On any other non-success response the agent apologises and asks the :Patient: to try again later; no :Appointment: is created.
- Finalize the :Call: when the :Patient: hangs up.
  - When the LiveKit room is disconnected from :VoiceAgent:'s perspective (the :Patient: left the room or the room was otherwise closed), :VoiceAgent: uses the language-model engine to write a one or two sentence English summary of the conversation that captures the :Patient:'s primary question and any booking outcome.
  - :VoiceAgent: then sends `POST /api/calls/<call_id>/end` to :WebApp: with body `{"summary": "<generated summary>", "appointmentId": "<stored appointment id, or null>"}`.
  - After the end call returns (or after a 5-second timeout if the call hangs), :VoiceAgent: releases all resources for that session and is ready to handle further LiveKit dispatch signals.
